    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d321">

                <pb facs="0543" n="441" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg441"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.114 Bodo/12: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Norway</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Biddle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Oslo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-11-22T18:00:00+01:00">November 22,
                            1935—6 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 22—1:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>27. Department’s telegram No. 17, November 18, 5 p.m.<anchor n="77"
                        xml:id="d321-f1"/> and No. 18, November 20, 5 p.m. Owner of Steamship <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bodo</hi> and Oslo agents have been placed on notice by
                    Norwegian Minister of Commerce and <pb facs="0544" n="442" xml:id="pg442"/> by
                    American Consulate General that Shaw the charterer of vessel may endeavor to use
                    it for purposes illegal under Norwegian law despite provisional time contract
                    providing that <hi rendition="#italic">Bodo</hi> shall not be used for unlawful
                    purposes. Oslo agents promise to warn captain to resist any attempt to use
                    vessel for smuggling.</p>
                <p>I have approached proper officials of the Ministry Foreign Affairs who have
                    induced above action by Minister of Commerce. However, the Foreign Office doubts
                    authority under the law to instruct Norwegian Consul General Antwerp to withhold
                    sailing documents from <hi rendition="#italic">Bodo</hi> in the absence
                    guarantee that cargo will be landed at declared destination. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bodo</hi> now at Newcastle, England but is about to
                    proceed to Antwerp. While I have met no categorical refusal from the Foreign
                    Office of further cooperation I believe that its officials will not take further
                    action in the hope that owner <hi rendition="#italic">Bodo</hi> will cancel
                    contract or demand guarantee from Shaw.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Biddle</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
